00:00:07.579 --> 00:00:12.330
In 2007, Steve Jobs introduced the original
iPhone.

00:00:12.330 --> 00:00:16.810
One of the most innovative and revolutionary
products of the 21st century, ushering a new

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.750
era of smartphones, and waiting in line to
get one.

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:24.450
The furore for this new phone was due, in
large part, to the LCD multi-touch panel that

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:29.110
allowed you to see and interact with the device
with a clarity and mobility never seen before.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.980
Apple wasn’t the first to introduce touch
panel LCDs into cell phones, but they were

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:38.210
first to make people care about it, they revolutionised
what was once a clunky and frustrating technology,

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:41.750
to one that could be used easily for typing
on screen and gesture control.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:47.010
The screen itself featured a 3.5” diagonal
320x480 resolution LCD.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.330
Most major shifts in iPhone usability and
design over the years have been around the

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:56.719
display, from the iPhone to the iPhone 3GS
the phones had a display density of 163 pixels

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:57.860
per inch.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:02.200
The iPhone 4 “retina” display doubled
that to 326 PPI, halving the size each pixel.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:06.400
This pixel size remained constant for another
7 years, while generational progress was made

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.970
in LCD technology.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:12.020
More consistent backlighting, consistent colour
and brightness when viewing off angle, and

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.720
a wider colour gamut gave the new iPhone 8
the gold standard LCD panel.

00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.590
But for the iPhone X, Apple is taking a sudden
departure from its faithful LCD screens, and

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:26.630
jumping in bed with their arch-rival, Samsung,
who are manufacturing it’s new OLED display.

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:30.620
The Apple watch used OLED, but Samsung has
been utilizing and maturing OLED technology

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.119
for years in their Galaxy smartphones.

00:01:33.119 --> 00:01:36.530
This is the first time most Apple enthusiasts
will experience OLED technology.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:40.810
So, what are the fundamental differences between
LCD and OLED?

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:43.369
LCD stands for liquid crystal display.

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:47.420
The liquid crystals used, fall somewhere in
between a liquid and a solid, which allows

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.950
them to change their molecular orientation
when an electrical current is applied.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:56.040
This is incredibly useful,as it allows us
to use the molecules to manipulate light that

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.479
is passing through the liquid crystal.

00:01:58.479 --> 00:02:00.880
Liquid crystal pixels do not produce their
own light, and so require a backlight to provide

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:01.880
the displays lighting.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:02.880
In the past, cold cathode fluorescent lamps
(CCLF) were used, but in modern LCD panels

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.110
LED backlighting is used with a backlight
uniformity filter added to ensure even lighting

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.580
across the panel.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.150
Next the light passes through a polarization
film which only lets light traveling in the

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:16.919
horizontal orientation to pass through, followed
by the liquid crystal group and then another

00:02:16.919 --> 00:02:20.290
polarizing filter oriented 90 degrees to the
first.

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:24.159
If you are familiar with polarizing filters,
you will know that light cannot pass through

00:02:24.159 --> 00:02:26.819
two filters when they are aligned perpendicular
to each other.

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:31.030
So, light depends on this liquid crystal layer
to orientate the light to allow it to pass

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.709
through the second filter.

00:02:32.709 --> 00:02:37.629
The liquid crystal layer is sandwiched between
two electrodes and two pieces of etched glass,

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:41.559
forcing the liquid crystal molecules into
a near predictable 90 degree twist.

00:02:41.559 --> 00:02:45.760
When a voltage is applied through the electrodes,
the crystal molecules begin to realign in

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:50.639
the direction of the current, a larger voltage
will result in full 90 degree twist.

00:02:50.639 --> 00:02:54.139
As light passes through, it will follow the
direction of the crystal molecules before

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:56.769
encountering the second polarization filter.

00:02:56.769 --> 00:03:00.239
So the liquid crystal layer essentially acts
as a controllable light valve.

00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:03.989
If light is allowed to pass it will travel
through a coloured filter, giving it one of

00:03:03.989 --> 00:03:04.989
three colours.

00:03:04.989 --> 00:03:06.540
Red, blue or green.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:08.700
This structure is called a subpixel.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.079
Combine three sub-pixels with red green and
blue filters and you have a single traditional

00:03:13.079 --> 00:03:15.319
RGB liquid crystal pixel.

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:18.959
This brings up a few really interesting characteristics
of LCDs.

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:23.689
The first is that even when the panel is displaying
a black image, the backlight is always on,

00:03:23.689 --> 00:03:27.549
causing a large battery drain and some light
leakage at pixel boundaries and the edges

00:03:27.549 --> 00:03:31.299
of the screen, which negatively affects the
screen contrast ratio.

00:03:31.299 --> 00:03:36.569
With the iPhone X, Apple engineers have adopted
a fundamentally new display technology, OLED

00:03:36.569 --> 00:03:39.230
or organic light emitting diodes.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:43.870
Samsung have a near perfect monopoly on the
manufacturing market for these screens, manufacturing

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:48.089
90% of the world’s supply and holding the
majority of the world’s patents [1].

00:03:48.089 --> 00:03:51.629
Forcing competitors like Apple, to purchase
from them to remain up to date with the best

00:03:51.629 --> 00:03:56.139
technology on the market, although it hasn't
stopped them from reportedly investing billions

00:03:56.139 --> 00:03:59.140
in Samsung's major competitor, LG Display
[2].

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.519
OLED works very differently than LCD in a
few key ways.

00:04:02.519 --> 00:04:04.189
For starters, there is no backlight.

00:04:04.189 --> 00:04:09.749
Instead, each pixel is self illuminating through
the light emitting process called electrophosphorescence.

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:14.109
There are two organic compounds called the
emissive and conductive layers that are sandwiched

00:04:14.109 --> 00:04:16.000
between an anode and a cathode.

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.620
As an electrical current flows from the cathode
to the anode, electrons are given to the emissive

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.699
layer, and removed from the conductive layer.

00:04:22.699 --> 00:04:26.840
The electrons in the emissive layer now migrate
to fill the holes in the conductive layer.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.830
When they do, extra energy is released as
a photon.

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:35.050
This entire process is controlled and structured
by the TFT matrix, and the structure is placed

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.580
on a substrate of either glass or polymer.

00:04:37.580 --> 00:04:42.260
Since there is no backlight and each individual
pixel is individually controlled, substantial

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:44.990
power saving can occur when displaying dark
or black images.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:51.080
In addition, OLED panels are significantly
thinner and lighter than their LCD counterparts,

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:55.289
allowing for slimmer devices with razor sharp
bezels and flexible screens, like the one

00:04:55.289 --> 00:04:57.180
in the Samsung Galaxy S8.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.039
Lastly, OLED panels have high brightness and
true zero pixel values, leading to insane

00:05:02.039 --> 00:05:04.789
contrast ratios of over 1,000,000:1.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:09.400
Blacks are inky, colors are more vibrant,
and localized brightness can be greatly improved.

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:13.479
There are some growing pains with OLED technology
holding it back in the mainstream consumer

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:14.479
market.

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:15.979
The chief limiting factor is burn in.

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:20.039
Burn in occurs when the organic layers of
the pixel begin to breakdown from prolonged

00:05:20.039 --> 00:05:21.039
usage.

00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:25.099
As the layers decay, they can become significantly
less bright than the pixels around them, which

00:05:25.099 --> 00:05:28.560
can make stationary UI elements appear as
a ghost image.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:33.199
This mainly occurs from prolonged static images,
such as user interface icons and is the single

00:05:33.199 --> 00:05:37.350
largest reasons OLED computer monitors or
TVs are not yet mainstream.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:42.289
Not to mention the cost, which can be 1.5
to 2 times higher than an equivalent LCD panel.

00:05:42.289 --> 00:05:46.790
OLED has fundamental strengths over LCD that
will open up the possibilities for how and

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:49.410
where displays are used in the world around
us.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:55.249
LG Display has a consumer OLED television
that is only 2.57mm thick, they are working

00:05:55.249 --> 00:05:59.919
on transparent OLEDs that can integrated into
glass panels, and various OLED manufacturers

00:05:59.919 --> 00:06:02.159
are working towards foldable displays.

00:06:02.159 --> 00:06:06.249
With continued improvement OLED displays look
set to revolutionise the way we consume and

00:06:06.249 --> 00:06:09.009
view information in the world around us.

